# Copy this file to `.env` (repo root, gitignored) on the server and set a strong password
# before running deploy/bootstrap-server.sh — the checked-in docker-compose.yml default
# (pms_demo_dev) is fine for local dev only, never for the public VPS.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   sed -i "s#change-me-to-a-long-random-value#$(openssl rand -base64 24)#" .env
#
# This is the ONE file you edit by hand for the Demo database. `.env` (this exact name, in
# the same directory as docker-compose.yml) is Docker Compose's own auto-discovered default
# env file — every `docker compose ...` invocation picks it up automatically, no --env-file
# flag required, so there's no way to accidentally run a command that falls back to the
# checked-in dev default. deploy/bootstrap-server.sh reads this same value and derives
# /var/www/pms-demo/shared/pms-demo.env's ConnectionStrings__Pm from it automatically — you never
# enter this password a second time anywhere.
#
# Changing this AFTER the postgres-demo volume already has data does nothing by itself —
# PostgreSQL only applies POSTGRES_PASSWORD the first time it initializes an empty data
# directory. To actually rotate the password, also wipe the volume — see deploy/RUNBOOK.md
# "Recovering from 'password authentication failed for user pms_demo'" — and re-run
# bootstrap-server.sh.

POSTGRES_DEMO_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-value
